1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
 So now let's talk about Amazon bedrock agents.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:06,360
 So the agent is going to be a very smart thing

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,400
 that is going to act a little bit like a human.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,400
 The idea is that instead of just asking questions

5
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,120
 to a model, now the model is going to be able to start

6
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:17,000
 thinking a little bit and to perform various multi-step tasks.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,200
 And these tasks may have an impact on our own databases

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:22,520
 or our own infrastructure.

9
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,280
 So the agent can actually create infrastructure.

10
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,120
 It can deploy applications and can do operations

11
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:29,400
 on our systems.

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,600
 So here now the agent doesn't provide us information.

13
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,000
 It also starts to think and act.

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,320
 So for example, it's going to look at tasks

15
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,480
 and then it's going to perform the task in the correct order

16
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,120
 and ensure that the correct information

17
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:43,760
 is passed within the task even if we haven't programmed

18
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:45,240
 the agent to do so.

19
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,960
 So what we do is that we are going to create

20
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,800
 what's called action groups.

21
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:52,480
 And the agent are going to be configured

22
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:56,600
 to understand what these action groups do and what they mean.

23
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:58,120
 And then automatically, the agent

24
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,680
 will be able to integrate with other systems, services databases

25
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,560
 and API to exchange data or to initiate action.

26
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:06,760
 And also, if it needs to get some information

27
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:10,320
 out of your systems in terms of unlabeled data,

28
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:12,760
 it can look at RAG to retrieve the information

29
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:14,120
 when necessary.

30
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,080
 So that sounds a little bit magical,

31
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:17,240
 but I will show you exactly how that works.

32
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,280
 So in Amazon Bedrock, you would go and create an agent

33
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,480
 and you are defining what the agent is responsible for.

34
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:25,440
 So for example, you are an agent responsible

35
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:28,040
 for accessing purchase history for our customers

36
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:31,040
 as well as recommendations into what they can purchase next.

37
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:33,480
 And you are responsible for placing new orders.

38
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:35,680
 So the agent knows that it can do all these things.

39
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,200
 So if the user is asking something for the agent

40
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:41,040
 or the model to do one of these things,

41
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:42,120
 Bedrock is smart is going to say,

42
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,360
 well, this agent probably is going to be responsible

43
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:45,600
 for these actions.

44
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,160
 Then the agent knows about a few action groups.

45
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:49,680
 So for example, we have defined an API,

46
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,360
 it's a way to interface with our system.

47
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,240
 And we have, for example, defined get recent purchases

48
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:58,160
 or get recommended purchases or get purchase details

49
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:00,400
 and then a specific purchase ID.

50
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,680
 So all these things are known to the agent

51
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:06,880
 in terms of what is the expected input for these APIs

52
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,240
 and what do these APIs do?

53
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:09,720
 What is the documentation around it?

54
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:12,920
 And all this is provided thanks to an open API schema.

55
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:16,240
 And so when done, well, the agent can invoke these

56
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:18,720
 and behind the scenes, of course, interact

57
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:19,600
 with our backend systems.

58
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:22,240
 For example, make changes to our database.

59
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:24,320
 The other way to set up an action group

60
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:25,520
 is to use Lambda functions.

61
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,520
 So Lambda functions are a way to run a little bit of code

62
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,440
 in AWS without provisioning infrastructure.

63
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:35,040
 So the Lambda functions again can be used to be created

64
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:37,560
 and place an order through Lambda function.

65
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:40,240
 And so it could use the same database or a new database.

66
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:42,280
 But the idea is that I wanted to show here

67
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,720
 that the agent can interact either with an external API

68
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:48,400
 or with Lambda functions on your AWS accounts.

69
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,600
 And finally, it has access to knowledge bases

70
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:51,960
 that we define, of course.

71
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,000
 And so for example, say we have a knowledge base

72
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,840
 around our company, a shipping policy

73
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,520
 and return policy, et cetera, et cetera.

74
00:02:58,520 --> 00:02:59,560
 So we could have those.

75
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:03,240
 And so if the user is asking something about the return policy

76
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:04,640
 for an order it's about to do,

77
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:08,120
 the agent is smart enough to also provide that to the user.

78
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:10,320
 So the agents are very smart and they know what to access

79
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:12,280
 and then automatically we'll know how to do it.

80
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:13,880
 So how does that work behind the scenes?

81
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,600
 Well, say we have a task.

82
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:18,000
 And we give this task to a bedrock agent.

83
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,240
 Now the agent is going to look at the prompt.

84
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:22,720
 He looks into look at all the conversation history,

85
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:24,880
 look at all the actions available,

86
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:26,600
 as well as the knowledge bases.

87
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:28,240
 What are these structures and what is the task?

88
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:31,560
 I'm just going to take all this information together

89
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:34,760
 and send it to a generative AI model

90
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,280
 backed by Amazon bedrock.

91
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:39,080
 And say, please tell me how you would proceed

92
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:42,280
 to perform these actions given all this information.

93
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:43,800
 So it's using the chain of thoughts.

94
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,480
 So in the fact means that the output of the bedrock model

95
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:48,400
 is going to be a list of steps.

96
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:49,720
 So step one into this.

97
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:50,760
 Step two, do this.

98
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:54,160
 Step three, do this and step n, last step, do that.

99
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,880
 And so the steps are going to be executed by the agents.

100
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,760
 And so first one, call an API call on this action group

101
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:00,600
 and get the results.

102
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,240
 Step two, do it against step three, call another API,

103
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:04,400
 et cetera, et cetera.

104
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:06,600
 Maybe you could use search into a knowledge base

105
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:07,600
 and they get the results and so on.

106
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:09,280
 But so the agent is going to work

107
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:12,720
 and do all these things for us, thanks to the steps

108
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,360
 that were generated by the bedrock model, which is amazing.

109
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,840
 And then the final result is returned to the bedrock agent.

110
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:21,880
 The bedrock agent then sends the tasks and the results

111
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:24,000
 to another bedrock model.

112
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,840
 And the bedrock model is going to synthesize everything

113
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:28,920
 and give a final response to our user

114
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:30,560
 and we'll get the final response.

115
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,480
 So this is all happening behind the scenes.

116
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:34,640
 Of course us, we'll just use the agent and the agent

117
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:37,400
 that stuff and automatically we see the final response.

118
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:39,160
 The bedrock is really nice because you actually

119
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:41,240
 something called tracing on your agent

120
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:43,840
 and this allows you to see the list of steps

121
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:45,040
 that were done by the agent.

122
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,320
 So you can debug in case you don't like a way

123
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,760
 an agent performed something.

124
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:51,920
 So that's it for Amazon bedrock agents.

125
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:54,920
 I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

